_ready = {"id": "ready", "nav": "Keeping a patient ready", "tag": "Practice", "h2": "Keep a waiting patient ready for the call",
 "html": """
<p>A patient who has been approved can lose a transplant opportunity on the day it comes: the phone number has changed, an infection is untreated, a transfusion two months ago created new antibodies that the program never heard about, or the patient missed sessions and arrives with a dangerous potassium. Keeping patients ready is mostly unglamorous follow-up, and it is where the liaison earns her place.</p>
<div class="tc-cards">
<div class="tc-card"><b>Reachable</b>Two working phone numbers for the patient and a family contact, checked every quarter and after any move.</div>
<div class="tc-card"><b>Transfusions reported</b>Every unit of blood, in any hospital, reported to the program with the date, because it can raise HLA antibodies.[[txcand2020]]</div>
<div class="tc-card"><b>Infections and admissions</b>Reported promptly; the program decides whether the patient stays active.</div>
<div class="tc-card"><b>Tests kept current</b>The program's list (blood samples for antibody testing, cardiac, dental, cancer screening as requested) tracked with dates and owners.</div>
<div class="tc-card"><b>Dialysis kept well</b>Attendance and fluid and potassium control matter on the day of the call; missed sessions are raised, not ignored.</div>
<div class="tc-card"><b>Still wants it</b>Goals change. Ask, at least yearly, whether the patient still wants a transplant, and tell the program if the answer changes.</div>
</div>
<div class="tc-scene"><span class="tc-lab">The transfusion nobody mentioned &middot; fictional</span><p>Mrs. Teresita Lim, 47, was approved for a living-donor transplant from her brother, with surgery planned in six weeks. During her quarterly check, Nurse Liaison Bea reads the discharge summary from a recent admission for a bleeding ulcer. Mrs. Lim received two units of packed red cells. Nobody told the transplant program; Mrs. Lim thought "the hospital would send it."</p><p>Bea calls the program's recipient coordinator that day, gives the dates and the hospital, and sends the discharge summary through the program's agreed secure channel. The program repeats the antibody test and the crossmatch before setting the date. Bea adds "any transfusion, anywhere: call the unit" to Mrs. Lim's written instructions and checks with teach-back that she can say it in her own words.</p><p class="tc-scene-take"><b>What this shows.</b> A transfusion can change a patient's antibody profile in ways that matter for crossmatching. The liaison's value is in noticing it and passing it on the same day; the program decides what testing to repeat.</p></div>
<div class="tc-plain"><span class="tc-lab">Role boundary</span>The liaison keeps information flowing and the checklist current. She does not decide whether a patient stays active, interpret crossmatch results, or tell a patient that a transfusion "won't matter."</div>
"""}
